                    coach_r2_good:          `<strong>That is the disciplined reallocation.</strong> A real brand floor protects the awareness that subsidises CAC, while a balanced, high-intent digital mix moves share and conversion. You are treating brand and performance as complements with different time horizons — exactly the 60/40 logic the board needs to see.`,
                    coach_r3_replatform:    `<strong>Funding the replatform is backing the leverage play.</strong> Conversion is the highest-value number in the building — at fixed traffic, moving toward 2.6% nearly doubles online revenue. This is where digital share is actually won, not on more spend.`,
                    coach_r3_partial:       `<strong>Your replatform stalled at partial funding.</strong> You tried to fund breadth and starved the one build with the most leverage — conversion crept to ~1.6% instead of 2.4%+. A half-built foundation carries none of the load. Which single investment would have moved revenue most, and why did you starve it?`,
                    coach_r3_marketplacefirst: `<strong>You grabbed the Allegro discount and skipped the replatform.</strong> Fast revenue, thin margin, and your own site still converts at 1.4%. The marketplace is demand capture you rent; the conversion foundation is the asset you own. You optimised for speed over leverage.`,
                    coach_r3_spread:        `<strong>You funded a little of everything.</strong> Breadth keeps every stakeholder happy and starves the leverage — the replatform stalls and conversion barely moves. Sequencing is the discipline: foundational before nice-to-have. Which one investment would have moved revenue most?`,
                    coach_r3_foundation:    `<strong>Foundation first is the disciplined sequence.</strong> The conversion replatform plus attribution compounds — the rest stacks on a foundation that converts and a measurement layer that proves which channels paid. You resisted the Allegro temptation for the asset you own.`,
                    coach_r3_noattrib:      `<strong>Conversion improved — congratulations. Now: which channels drove the new revenue?</strong> You skipped attribution, so you cannot say. Next year’s 64 mln zł has nowhere to point. Measurement capability is what protects marketing’s seat at the table — and you left it on the shelf.`,
                    coach_r4_causal:        `<strong>That is the defense a board trusts.</strong> A causal scorecard — which decision moved each KPI, proven with attribution, and the one you would reverse — is exactly what the Chair asked for. You are not asserting marketing’s return; you are demonstrating it.`,
                    coach_r4_causalnoattrib: `<strong>You want to defend causality — but you skipped attribution.</strong> The narrative is right; the evidence is missing. Without the measurement layer you can assert which decision moved the number but you cannot prove it, and assertion does not survive this room.`,
                    coach_r4_hitnumber:     `<strong>“We hit 20%, move on” invites the obvious probe.</strong> The Chair can see all five numbers. Point only at the headline and downplay how you got there, and the board assumes the rest is worse than it is — and that you cannot prove the win was marketing’s doing.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the market and the legacy platform is the CMO abdicating the mandate.</strong> The board hired you to lead the pivot through exactly those constraints. The platform was yours to replatform; the channels were yours to own. Causes you blame are the work you were given.`,
                    coach_r4_caconly:       `<strong>You drove CAC down — but on discount-seekers.</strong> You won the acquisition metric with price-shoppers whose order value is low and who never return. Acquisition cost is meaningless without lifetime value. What should you have measured alongside CAC?`,
                    coach_r4_shareonly:     `<strong>Digital share alone is half a scorecard.</strong> Volume answers the mandate’s headline, but the board is asking about <em>return</em> — order value, retention and the margin behind the share. A number without its economics is not yet a defense.`,
                    coach_r4_align:         `<strong>Aligning brand and digital under one P&L is the change-leadership move.</strong> A marketing transformation is also an organisational one — keep the team that holds 30 years of category knowledge and you keep the bench that executes the plan. You won the budget argument without losing the people.`,
                    coach_r4_winargument:   `<strong>You won the budget argument and lost the team.</strong> Two senior brand leaders walked, taking decades of category knowledge with them. Your performance plan is intact; your brand bench is empty. Was winning the argument worth losing the people who execute it?`,
                    coach_r4_ignoreteam:    `<strong>Leaving the conflict to HR was a choice, and it cost you.</strong> A transformation is a change-leadership problem; the CMO owns the coalition, not just the channels. Unmanaged, the brand-vs-digital conflict quietly drained the team you needed.`,

